focus on applications to aging-related research.
Emily Slade is an assistant professor of Biostatistics at the University of Kentucky and the founder and director of the Biostatistics Consulting
and Interdisciplinary Research Collaboration Lab (Biostat CIRCL). Her research focuses on effective training and integration of biostatisticians
on collaborative research teams.
Lacey Rende is the project planner for the Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) Methods Core at Duke University, a data
science unit of over 50 student, staff, and faculty biostatisticians and data scientists who collaborate on biomedical research projects with
clinical and translational researchers across the institution.
Mary Boulos is the executive director of the Quantitative Sciences Unit within the Department of Medicine at Stanford University, a data sci
ence unit of over 50 data scientist faculty, staff, and trainees created primarily for the purposes of practicing data science/team science and
providing the statistical foundation for interdisciplinary and collaborative translational research. She is also the operations lead for the CTSA
funded Biostatistics, Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) Program at Stanford University.
Manisha Desai is the Kim and Ping Li Professor of Medicine, Biomedical Data Science, and Epidemiology and Population Health at Stanford
